  "_id": "6a7f5797757ee51246381b1b",
  "lab": "6a7f5796eda1a5feaff0b458",
  "name": "URINE ALBUMIN",
  "code": "ALBUMIN",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<ul><li>Normal: A normal result indicates that there is no or only a trace amount of albumin in the urine, which is typically less than 30 milligrams per gram of creatinine. This is a normal result and indicates that the kidneys are functioning properly and that there is no significant damage to the kidneys.</li><li>Microalbuminuria: A result indicating microalbuminuria is between 30 and 300 milligrams of albumin per gram of creatinine. This may indicate early kidney damage or indicate risk for developing kidney disease. It is a sign that kidney function may not be optimal, and further evaluation may be needed to prevent further damage.</li><li>Macroalbuminuria: A result indicating macroalbuminuria is greater than 300 milligrams of albumin per gram of creatinine. This may indicate severe kidney damage or disease, such as advanced-stage chronic kidney disease, glomerulonephritis, or nephrotic syndrome.&nbsp;</li></ul>",
